00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:49.910
It's as if he lost his wife for 50 years.

257
00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.550
Paul Lieberstein wrote a lot.

258
00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:54.430
I love that episode.

259
00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:57.750
Paul is the source of the darkness on that one.

260
00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:02.030
And look, I wrote a lot of very joyous stuff too.

261
00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:04.090
I wrote a lot of The Jim and Pam.

262
00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:09.670
Um, I wrote, uh, Threat Level Midnight, which is a very joyful episode.

263
00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:14.230
Um, so I, I'm not, it's not like I'm the Prince of Darkness on the show.

264
00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:18.010
Um, and also look, there were what, how many hundred episodes?

265
00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:21.230
I worked on like, you know, more than half of them.

266
00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:25.630
So it's not, I'm, I'm not just this, this dark figure.

267
00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:26.690
I'm proud of it.

268
00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:31.850
I'm less dark now though, because again, I do hear this and I do go, oh my God.

269
00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.490
Like, how was I capable of some of this?

270
00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:38.810
Like, especially when I, when I see Ryan Howard, I'm like, oh my God.

271
00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:41.110
Like, I don't see any villain like this.

272
00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:44.850
Here's the Mashable article that comes out after this episode.

273
00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:48.630
BJ Novak, Prince of Darkness of The Office.

274
00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:52.770
No, BJ, we, we love like, my God.

275
00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:54.230
No, I think the dark is important.

276
00:12:54.230 --> 00:13:01.190
And I think, I think that, um, you know, I remember after season one, which was six episodes

277
00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:05.230
and I think all of them ended pretty darkly, right?

278
00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:07.530
The pilot, hot girl.

279
00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:11.850
I mean, we had that nice moment at the end of diversity, but, um, the pilot diversity

280
00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:17.610
day, hot girl, healthcare, extremely dark, um, basketball.

281
00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:18.750
I don't remember.

282
00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:23.970
Office Olympics and basketball maybe were a little more positive endings, but, you know,

283
00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:28.570
we had sort of a big group meeting that Greg led about, okay, what are we going to do

284
00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:29.650
differently in season two?

285
00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:30.450
And there was a lot different.

286
00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:37.130
And one of them was three out of four endings should be positive.

287
00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.950
And then one is just for us, meaning Paul.

288
00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:46.370
Um, he was like, we're not going to totally sell out, but the British had, had ended dark

289
00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.170
every time, more or less.

290
00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:50.350
And we had been ending dark every time, more or less.

291
00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:56.610
And so the new thing was, you know, so I guess Scott's Tots was definitely one of those four.

292
00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:57.470
One for us.

293
00:13:58.010 --> 00:13:58.410
Yeah.

294
00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:03.210
But again, I do, I do think it's an interesting, it is a deep, interesting one.

295
00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.430
Um, it's not that it's not part of the canon.

296
00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:07.690
It's, it is very difficult.

297
00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:08.310
I agree.

298
00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:08.810
I agree.

299
00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:11.450
I think though, it is Michael.

300
00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:18.830
It's honestly, Michael trying to do something good, believing in himself, knowing, well,

301
00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.530
I, I don't think he knows he's going to fall short.

302
00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:23.130
Well, look how much he believes in Pam and her, her art.

303
00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:23.770
I know.

304
00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:25.750
He is, he is such a believer.

305
00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:26.570
He is.

306
00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:27.050
You know?

307
00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:33.990
Um, and so his belief got ahead of him and this is by far, you know, exactly like the line

308
00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:39.550
he quoted, this is the biggest consequence of all, of all of his belief, you know?

309
00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:39.990
Yeah.

310
00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:42.930
Now I, we should remind people or I should remind people.

311
00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:45.150
I, I wish I had, I admire it.

312
00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:46.250
I did not write the episode.

313
00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:50.250
This was a, a Lee Eisenberg and Gene Sabnitsky episode.

314
00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:54.450
Um, and I hope it doesn't sound like I'm disowning it either because I know we talked about so

315
00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:55.130
many people are uncomfortable.

316
00:14:55.590 --> 00:15:00.850
You know, these are two of, I think the most brilliant, you know, writers backbone of the

317
00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:05.650
show when a lot of the original staff was burnt out.

318
00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:11.870
These guys took the ball and a lot of the sort of center central seasons of the, of the

319
00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:14.310
office that people that were is really humming.

320
00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:18.750
That was Lee and Gene were just turning out scripts and revising scripts.

321
00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:19.730
They're not even credited for.

322
00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:24.210
So those guys were really, um, an incredible backstop for that.

323
00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:29.910
But you know, the, look, Gene in particular, I think is the best one liner writer in office

324
00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:30.250
history.

325
00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:34.350
He's the one who wrote, uh, I'm not superstitious, but I'm a little stitious, you know?

326
00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:36.550
So I, I would guess he wrote that line.

327
00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:43.190
Um, but yeah, I mean, maybe we're getting a little off track, but that is sort of this

328
00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:51.510
incredible distillation of why we love Michael so much and why he's made everyone's life so

329
00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:56.670
impossibly difficult, which is sort of the heart and the comedy of the series, but it's

330
00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.190
not usually that intense, you know?

331
00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:01.230
Well, what was it like BJ?

332
00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:05.910
You talked about, um, you know, Lee and Gene as the writers, but you went from writing to

333
00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.490
directing on the office.

334
00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:11.770
And what was that shift like to go from the writer's room to being the director of the

335
00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:12.210
episode?

336
00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.470
It felt very intuitive.

337
00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:17.290
I took it very seriously, but I, I overprepared.

338
00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:20.770
I don't think I did any better on the first one than I did on the fifth one that I did.

339
00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:23.070
But I think that it's just very interesting.

340
00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:27.490
What is required to leap from writer to director?

341
00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:35.570
And I think to an extent, actor to director is so much the confidence to picture yourself

342
00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:37.370
saying action and cut.

343
00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:42.510
Did you get any advice from anyone before you started directing?

344
00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:48.530
Yes, I got the best advice and I pass it on to anyone who directs.

345
00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:52.110
So the first piece of advice I got, first of all, it's good advice in general.

346
00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:53.270
Ask everyone for advice.

347
00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:54.990
People love giving advice.

348
00:16:54.990 --> 00:17:00.350
Um, so anytime you wish you had advice in your show, I ask them, people will talk your ear

349
00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:00.610
off.

350
00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:03.630
So I asked everybody, what's your advice for directing?

351
00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:10.390
And I should also, we should clarify directing an ongoing TV show is incredibly different.

352
00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:16.770
The actors know the characters, the writers know the story, the, uh, uh, directors of photography

353
00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:18.410
and cameramen know how to shoot it.

354
00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:24.530
If you just stand there and nicely and intuitively and confidently ask everyone, how would you shoot

355
00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:24.730
this?

356
00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:25.930
How would you schedule this?

357
00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:27.870
Did that feel good to you?

358
00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:28.370
You're fine.

359
00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:31.190
Because the show is set in the pilot stage.

360
00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:36.990
Whereas a movie or a limited series, which is essentially like a long movie you have to

361
00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:37.530
establish.

362
00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:40.010
Is there music in the show?

363
00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:43.050
Is this a very colorful show?

364
00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:46.870
Is this a little bit of a campy wink wink show?

365
00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:48.250
Is it super deadpan?

366
00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:52.590
Like you have to establish all these things, but on an ongoing show, it's kind of like,

367
00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:56.830
I look, I took it super seriously because it's an amazing first step, but it's not nearly

368
00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:59.350
as hard, you know, coming into an ongoing show.

369
00:17:59.630 --> 00:17:59.810
Okay.

370
00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:06.350
The advice I got from Randall Einhorn, our longtime director of photography and later our most,

371
00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:08.410
um, prolific director on the show.

372
00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.190
I said, do you have advice?

373
00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:14.230
He said, yes, direct the cameras like they're actors.

374
00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:16.370
I said, what do you mean?

375
00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:23.150
He said, you'd never tell an actor, shout this line and cut it off after three seconds.

376
00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:23.890
Right.

377
00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:25.170
You tell them why.

378
00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:31.170
So you never tell the camera swing to the left on this line and punch in on that.

379
00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:34.550
You'd say, this is where we catch Pam and her lie.

380
00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:39.970
So I would creep up to her and find that moment and don't let her out.

381
00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:40.930
You know what I mean?

382
00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.570
And it, it inspires so much more creativity.

383
00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:47.810
It's so much more respectful and you're more likely to get what you're looking for.

384
00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:52.790
And, you know, I've extended that to direct to everyone like their actors.

385
00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:55.470
You don't tell the costume department, I want him in a suit.

386
00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:01.290
You say, this is a character who's so insecure that he takes himself.

387
00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:03.590
He tries to tell the world he's important.

388
00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:04.590
I want him in a suit.

389
00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:06.030
Every day he'll wear a suit.

390
00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:10.270
Now the costume designer is so excited, you know, rather than, oh, she went to Sears and

391
00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:10.630
got a suit.

392
00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.390
And then you say, make it look fancier.

393
00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:14.070
No one understands what you're doing.

394
00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:14.830
No one's motivated.

395
00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:17.830
Whereas actors, that's how you talk to an actor, right?

396
00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:19.250
And people know that much more.

397
00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:24.170
So direct everyone like they're actors is I think the best advice I got on that show.

398
00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:29.810
Other advice I got later, the other two great pieces of advice, a friend, Lee Whannell, who

399
00:19:29.810 --> 00:19:32.550
just wrote and directed Invisible Man, which I thought was great.

400
00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:34.050
And he had been a writer forever.

401
00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:40.510
And he said that he actually took a panel on directing at the Writers Guild of America.

402
00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:42.490
And he's like, BJ, I won't do his Australian accent.

403
00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:45.970
He's like, it's the kind of thing you get junk mail for.

404
00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.470
Come to this seminar, blah, blah, blah.

405
00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:52.230
It was the best thing I've ever been to in my life.

406
00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:53.970
It was like John Wells.

407
00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:55.250
It was Catherine Bigelow.

408
00:19:55.410 --> 00:20:00.710
And they were giving practical advice and cynical advice and inspiring advice.

409
00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:03.450
And he was like, it was the best thing I've ever been to.

410
00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.870
And everything I tell you is going to be from that.

411
00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:10.370
This advice was the best thing you can do as a director is watch the movie in your head

412
00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:11.750
as much as you can.

413
00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:15.490
Whenever you're in doubt, just take a walk and watch the whole movie in your head.

414
00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:21.430
And then when you're in a production meeting and someone says, what does the room look

415
00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:21.770
like?

416
00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.950
You just say what you see and they figure it out.

417
00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:29.850
You don't need to know what the lens is and what the lighting scheme is.

418
00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:35.690
If someone says, you know, what are they wearing or how do you want to shoot this?

419
00:20:36.110 --> 00:20:41.170
You know, you can say, oh, I picture closeups or I picture, oh, you're like looking from

420
00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:41.670
a distance.

421
00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:47.990
You know, it will have the same effect as if you studied everything in school because the

422
00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:49.050
point is to know the heart.

423
00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:50.070
So watch the movie in your head.

424
00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:58.290
But the other piece of advice that's so simple, this was, I have a friend whose father is Al

425
00:20:58.290 --> 00:20:59.830
Ruddy who produced The Godfather.

426
00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:04.210
And I asked him if he had any advice on directing.

427
00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:10.430
He said, you only need to know two things to direct, what you want and how to get it.

428
00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:13.750
And I was like, you know what?

429
00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:20.830
I think that's the best advice I've gotten for anything because those are two very hard

430
00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:21.370
things.

431
00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:28.450
In a way, knowing what you want is even harder, but all of us can approach, okay, if you know

432
00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.970
what you want and how to get it, you'll accomplish anything.

433
00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:33.710
And we do that for all sorts of things.

434
00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:40.290
And how to get it includes asking someone for help or to understand something or to explain

435
00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:47.750
something or to beg them for a favor, you know, or to, you know, grind them down because

436
00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:53.030
they're just not getting it and whatever it is or inspiring them or whatever you need to

437
00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:53.450
get it.

438
00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.550
So that's, you know, what you want and how to get it.

439
00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:59.710
And then I told them, I ran into him and I was like, you gave me the best advice ever.

440
00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:00.410
And I quoted it to him.

441
00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:02.610
And he's like, oh, there's a third part.

442
00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:03.510
I forgot to tell you.

443
00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:05.690
You got to know when you got it.

444
00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:06.750
I was like, I don't like that one.

445
00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:08.750
I'm editing that one out.

446
00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:09.510
You're not wrong.

447
00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:11.510
You're not wrong, but it's not as good a quote.

448
00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:12.650
And I said that to him.

449
00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:14.130
He's like, no, no, I don't know what.

450
00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:16.110
I think he just worked on a movie with some director.

451
00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:16.670
It took forever.

452
00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:21.330
I was like, Al, no, this is not, this is Godfather part three.

453
00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:22.390
This is not as good.

454
00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:24.490
So I just quote the first two parts.

455
00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:25.730
That's so true, BJ.

456
00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:32.170
I know, you know, Angela and I changed our whole lives because we wanted to get something.

457
00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:34.330
We wanted to get a quality of life.

458
00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:36.470
We wanted to be able to make it to soccer practice.

459
00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:38.190
We want to take our kids to school.

460
00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:39.670
I didn't know you play soccer.

461
00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:40.950
Seriously.

462
00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:42.850
I'm sweeper, BJ.

463
00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:44.610
Nothing's getting into the goal.

464
00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:48.570
No, we just wanted to make our families firSt.

465
00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:58.010
And yeah, just have agency over our own schedules and our time and pour that into our family as a priority.

466
00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:00.510
And we didn't know how to get it.

467
00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:01.630
That took a long time.

468
00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:03.590
It took a lot of experimenting.

469
00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:06.590
Years of kind of griping to each other.

470
00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:07.130
Yeah.

471
00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:09.090
But then eventually we did it.

472
00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:16.830
And here we are interviewing you on our podcast, which is the result of our wanting to get something.

473
00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:18.870
And we didn't know how to do it.

474
00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:20.210
No, as you were talking.

475
00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.230
But it's so much easier to figure out how to do it.

476
00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:25.890
Like the worst person to work with is someone who doesn't know what they want.

477
00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:26.830
Oh, my God.

478
00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:27.490
What a nightmare.

479
00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:27.870
Right?

480
00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:28.270
Nightmare.

481
00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:29.050
You knew what you wanted.

482
00:23:29.050 --> 00:23:30.890
So now you're like, I have no idea how to get this.

483
00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:31.430
Yeah.

484
00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:32.790
But like you figure that out.

485
00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:33.850
But you're right.

486
00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:38.770
I've worked with directors who don't know what they want, and it really puts everybody through the ringer.

487
00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:44.190
You know, you're changing clothes a million times, and you're doing the role different ways.

488
00:23:44.510 --> 00:23:47.310
And everyone, it really runs people into the ground.

489
00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:53.230
But someone with a very clear vision, then you're on set and you're like, okay, well, how do we do it?

490
00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:54.690
How do we make this happen?

491
00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:56.850
Like, just give me something that I can hang on to.

492
00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:57.330
Right.

493
00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:59.630
If your vision is clear, we'll get there.

494
00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:09.130
Um, oh, I have one other very cynical piece of directing advice, and it does not make the top three, but it really worked.

495
00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:14.650
And someone said to me who had worked on The Office, said, here's my advice.

496
00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:18.530
Pick one shot that's a special shot.

497
00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:19.950
It doesn't have to make sense.

498
00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:22.450
It just like, it stands out.

499
00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:23.650
It doesn't quite fit.

500
00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:28.870
And the network will tell you, you're a great director, because they'll notice it and they'll feel proud.

501
00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:29.990
And I did that.

502
00:24:30.370 --> 00:24:31.950
I think it was maybe in the web episode.

503
00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:33.030
No, I think it was in this.

504
00:24:33.190 --> 00:24:37.350
There's a shot where Creed is like standing by the doorway and waiting to go into the room.

505
00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:39.030
I don't know which episode it was in.

506
00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:41.430
But literally, the network is that called me personally.

507
00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:43.970
It's like, BJ, you are a great director.

508
00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:45.270
And it was that shot.

509
00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:50.750
And it was like, it was a stretch that the, I mean, it didn't break The Office style.

510
00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:52.310
But that is a cynical thing.

511
00:24:52.450 --> 00:25:01.410
And definitely when you see, like, I've joked, the easiest way to win an Oscar is to be a cinematographer and decide to shoot the movie in glorious black and white.

512
00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:05.930
Like, anything that calls attention to yourself as a director is so rewarded.

513
00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:08.630
So that is my cynical advice.

514
00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:15.390
If you do want to, if you're, if you have the chance to direct something and you want people to think you're good, do something visually unusual,

515
00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:17.590
which will flatter the intelligence.

516
00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:19.310
Do one odd thing.

517
00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:23.870
And every critic and every person will say, ooh, you know, you're good.

518
00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:28.090
Whereas if you just shoot intuitively, like Harold Ramis, you know, is a great director.

519
00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:32.090
He directed a lot for us, but, you know, his movies are classics.

520
00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:36.110
But no one talks about him as a great director because he didn't call attention to himself.

521
00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:40.090
Rob Reiner, to me, is the greatest director of our lifetimes.

522
00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:46.150
Because if you look at the movies he directed, they are the classics of their genres.

523
00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:55.890
He did Spinal Tap, which is the classic mockumentary, Stand By Me, classic coming of age, Princess Bride, classic family movie, Few Good Men, classic.

524
00:25:56.350 --> 00:25:58.890
And I don't just mean classic, I mean the epitome of their genre.

525
00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:01.550
Harry Met Sally, epitome of romantic comedy.

526
00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:07.430
And people don't talk about him, you know, because he just did it so right that he was invisible.

527
00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:16.050
If he had had like, you know, like an overhead camera in the ceiling fan, Harry Met Sally, everyone say, and he's such a great director.

528
00:26:16.150 --> 00:26:17.570
But, you know.

529
00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:22.450
BJ, that reminds me of a piece of acting advice that I got that I use all the time.

530
00:26:22.650 --> 00:26:22.970
What?

531
00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:39.310
So, in any project, if it's a TV show or a movie, my acting coach said, you pick one moment for your character that you decide in this project is your big moment of realization,

532
00:26:39.890 --> 00:26:41.870
a confession, something.

533
00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:42.670
You pick one.

534
00:26:42.830 --> 00:26:51.270
And before you say that line, you pause, move forward, and then say it.

535
00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:53.830
And it will blow people away.

536
00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:54.310
Your sunglasses.

537
00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:54.830
Yeah.

538
00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:59.810
And he said, even in auditioning, if you have just an audition scene,

539
00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:07.230
you pick one line in that audition scene where you pause and then you lean in before you say that line and you'll get the part every time.

540
00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:13.510
I swear to God, after I got that piece of acting advice, I went on a bunch of auditions.

541
00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:18.630
I got callbacks for like 12 auditions in a row when I did that little move in thing.

542
00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:19.790
And I still use it.

543
00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:21.150
I still use that.

544
00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:21.690
That's amazing.

545
00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:22.450
Okay.

546
00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:23.510
This is so funny to me.

547
00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:24.450
Is that in your book?

548
00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:31.250
Um, it's not in my book because I didn't want to steal his thunder.

549
00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:32.010
Oh, got it.

550
00:27:32.170 --> 00:27:34.190
That's Robert Devonzo, my acting coach.

551
00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:37.210
And I put him in the book and I said, call this guy and take his class.

552
00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:38.330
He'll give you the best advice.

553
00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:40.070
But I guess now I've put it on my podcaSt.

554
00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:41.190
So, sorry, Robert.

555
00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:43.450
My acting coach did something similar.

556
00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:47.030
She was, um, this very amazing old, older woman.

557
00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:56.730
And she said that she was auditioning for a movie and she was from France and she had an old, um, crown, like some dental work that as she was giving her big speech,

558
00:27:56.870 --> 00:27:59.410
she felt pop off in her mouth.

559
00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:06.190
And so she paused with her tongue to put it back in to cover her tooth back.

560
00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:10.050
And the pause was so long, the room became quiet.

561
00:28:10.530 --> 00:28:15.270
And then once she got her crown back over her tooth, she finished her line.

562
00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:20.070
And the casting director said that was the most powerful audition we have ever seen.

563
00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:21.330
She got the part.

564
00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:25.730
And so she was like, so sometimes, you know, a pause.

565
00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:29.110
That's my French impression.

566
00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:33.330
But, and it does, it's like, it's the one moment that doesn't make sense.

567
00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:35.030
And people are like, why doesn't that make sense?

568
00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:40.230
That's, that's such a good way to distill the, the common thread of those two things.

569
00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:40.570
Yeah.

570
00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:41.130
Yeah.

571
00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:42.090
Well, okay.

