00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:14.230
She feels like she's not getting through to Michael.

981
00:48:14.410 --> 00:48:17.190
She's like, why would you promise something like that?

982
00:48:17.350 --> 00:48:19.530
This is a terrible thing, Michael.

983
00:48:19.770 --> 00:48:20.950
This is terrible.

984
00:48:21.290 --> 00:48:22.430
It's just terrible.

985
00:48:22.430 --> 00:48:25.010
And she's like, you have to go tell them now.

986
00:48:25.810 --> 00:48:31.690
Did you see the article, Angela, where it sort of broke down there are two reactions

987
00:48:31.690 --> 00:48:33.370
to this episode from viewers.

988
00:48:33.370 --> 00:48:36.210
You're either a Stanley or a Pam.

989
00:48:36.470 --> 00:48:41.490
Like it was saying, you're either a person who laughs through tragedy.

990
00:48:41.770 --> 00:48:42.050
Yeah.

991
00:48:42.050 --> 00:48:44.830
You know, you're that person at the funeral who gets the giggles.

992
00:48:45.090 --> 00:48:45.270
Right.

993
00:48:45.470 --> 00:48:48.390
Or you have no sense of humor about it.

994
00:48:48.710 --> 00:48:53.130
They were sort of saying like, if you're a Pam, you probably can't watch this episode.

995
00:48:53.330 --> 00:48:53.670
Right.

996
00:48:53.890 --> 00:48:55.930
It's a really interesting article, actually.

997
00:48:56.110 --> 00:48:57.370
It's on decider.com.

998
00:48:57.790 --> 00:49:00.090
And I don't know which one I am.

999
00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:02.010
I mean, I have a visceral reaction.

1000
00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:03.610
Like I feel so bad for these kids.

1001
00:49:03.790 --> 00:49:07.610
And I also see Stanley finally getting to see Michael get his comeuppance.

1002
00:49:08.130 --> 00:49:08.510
Yes.

1003
00:49:09.250 --> 00:49:11.230
I don't know which one I am either.

1004
00:49:11.230 --> 00:49:12.630
I enjoyed this episode.

1005
00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:14.830
So I guess I must be a little Stanley.

1006
00:49:15.670 --> 00:49:16.930
Maybe we're a combo.

1007
00:49:17.410 --> 00:49:18.930
I don't know who that would be.

1008
00:49:19.070 --> 00:49:20.070
Are you an Aaron then?

1009
00:49:20.790 --> 00:49:22.450
Aaron seems horrified.

1010
00:49:22.710 --> 00:49:27.810
And yet also, she still finds a positive spin at the end.

1011
00:49:28.250 --> 00:49:29.490
Yeah, maybe we're Aaron.

1012
00:49:29.910 --> 00:49:34.890
The article said about people who are Stanleys, and I thought this was worded really well.

1013
00:49:35.070 --> 00:49:36.410
They're not heartless.

1014
00:49:36.410 --> 00:49:39.510
They're almost shocked to the point of hilarity.

1015
00:49:39.810 --> 00:49:40.270
Right.

1016
00:49:40.570 --> 00:49:44.830
When something is so insanely absurd to you that someone could do that, that you're like, what?

1017
00:49:45.710 --> 00:49:47.130
That's their coping mechanism.

1018
00:49:47.310 --> 00:49:47.510
Yeah.

1019
00:49:47.850 --> 00:49:48.130
Right.

1020
00:49:48.750 --> 00:49:51.050
Whereas Pam, it's just all just torturous.

1021
00:49:51.750 --> 00:49:52.310
Yeah.

1022
00:49:53.270 --> 00:49:59.050
Well, I was really disappointed because I didn't get to go with Michael.

1023
00:49:59.210 --> 00:50:06.770
As an actor, I wanted to see Pam take Michael to this moment in his life.

1024
00:50:07.350 --> 00:50:09.350
But Pam refuses to go.

1025
00:50:10.430 --> 00:50:11.950
Aaron's going to have to do it.

1026
00:50:12.090 --> 00:50:15.090
But I was very jealous as an actor.

1027
00:50:15.330 --> 00:50:19.230
That you didn't get to go spend the day with Steve and watch him do these amazing scenes.

1028
00:50:19.230 --> 00:50:19.650
Yes.

1029
00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:22.510
Well, Michael now has this famous talking head.

1030
00:50:22.590 --> 00:50:27.250
We talked about it a little bit with BJ, where he says, I just, you know, I fell in love with those kids.

1031
00:50:27.710 --> 00:50:29.290
And so he made him a promise.

1032
00:50:29.450 --> 00:50:30.490
He wanted to help them.

1033
00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:34.550
He wanted to see them graduate from high school, and he would pay for their college.

1034
00:50:34.550 --> 00:50:41.710
Yes, this is sort of the explanation behind this predicament, behind the premise for this episode.

1035
00:50:41.990 --> 00:50:46.510
We got a fan question from Aaron in Oxford, Ohio, who said this.

1036
00:50:46.510 --> 00:50:53.590
I recently watched The Big Lebowski, and there is a specific scene that caught my eye.

1037
00:50:54.010 --> 00:50:58.330
In the scene, the dude is visiting Mr. Lebowski's house.

1038
00:50:58.730 --> 00:51:04.450
During the visit, he's given a tour of the study and notices a picture on the wall of Mr. Lebowski

1039
00:51:04.450 --> 00:51:07.830
surrounded by a bunch of smiling high school-age children.

1040
00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:13.030
The dude asks, oh, are those Mr. Lebowski's children?

1041
00:51:13.470 --> 00:51:17.450
To which the butler replies, they're not literally his children.

1042
00:51:17.490 --> 00:51:22.310
They're the little Lebowski urban achievers, inner-city children of promise, but without

1043
00:51:22.310 --> 00:51:24.610
the necessary means for a higher education.

1044
00:51:24.890 --> 00:51:28.930
So Mr. Lebowski has committed to sending all of them to college.

1045
00:51:28.930 --> 00:51:35.710
My question is, did the writers have it in their head that Michael saw this movie, and that

1046
00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:41.470
this might have led him to making a similar promise, or is it just a coincidence?

1047
00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:44.170
I had to find out.

1048
00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:50.530
So I reached out to Gene and Lee, who wrote this episode, and they said that the idea for

1049
00:51:50.530 --> 00:51:55.010
this episode actually came from Paul Lieberstein.

1050
00:51:55.650 --> 00:52:00.690
Paul confirmed this, and when I asked him if the idea was inspired by The Big Lebowski,

1051
00:52:00.730 --> 00:52:04.490
he said no, that he wasn't even aware of that scene in the movie.

1052
00:52:05.070 --> 00:52:10.610
Paul said that there was a real story of a businessman who did this with extraordinary

1053
00:52:10.610 --> 00:52:13.050
success back in the 1990s.

1054
00:52:13.170 --> 00:52:15.270
He thinks maybe it was Chicago.

1055
00:52:15.270 --> 00:52:16.690
He couldn't quite remember.

1056
00:52:17.170 --> 00:52:20.890
But it was a high school where the graduation rate was incredibly low.

1057
00:52:20.890 --> 00:52:26.370
But within the group of kids that he made this promise to, all of them went to college.

1058
00:52:26.850 --> 00:52:33.550
And Paul just remembered the photo in the newspaper, and he said that seems like something

1059
00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:35.390
Michael Scott would do.

1060
00:52:35.690 --> 00:52:41.090
That Michael, because of his good heart and being so sure that he would be a millionaire

1061
00:52:41.090 --> 00:52:48.190
by the time these kids graduated, that he would want to help, and he would make this promise.

1062
00:52:48.190 --> 00:52:53.250
He said, quote, it felt like a really honest problem coming from this character.

1063
00:52:53.890 --> 00:52:59.710
So that was the inspiration, was this article that he had seen of someone doing this successfully

1064
00:52:59.710 --> 00:53:04.250
and thinking, Michael Scott wouldn't do it successfully, though, would he?

1065
00:53:04.250 --> 00:53:06.910
He'd want to do it, but he wouldn't be able to follow through.

1066
00:53:07.170 --> 00:53:07.410
Yeah.

1067
00:53:07.550 --> 00:53:10.970
So he said the idea was his, but Lee and Gene made it their own.

1068
00:53:11.110 --> 00:53:13.270
They came up with the dance, the batteries.

1069
00:53:13.270 --> 00:53:14.810
He said that was all them.

1070
00:53:15.070 --> 00:53:18.950
But that was the inspiration behind Scott's Tots.

1071
00:53:19.570 --> 00:53:20.170
Well, I love that.

1072
00:53:20.250 --> 00:53:22.450
I always love finding out the inspiration for our episodes.

1073
00:53:22.850 --> 00:53:25.630
It's usually some little thing that just stuck with someone.

1074
00:53:25.850 --> 00:53:26.050
Yeah.

1075
00:53:26.590 --> 00:53:31.110
Meanwhile, in the kitchen, Dwight is continuing to act strange.

1076
00:53:32.170 --> 00:53:32.650
Yeah.

1077
00:53:32.650 --> 00:53:40.490
He's showering Jim with compliments, and then he provides him with a spreadsheet so that Jim

1078
00:53:40.490 --> 00:53:44.830
can rank everyone based on performance for his Employee of the Month project.

1079
00:53:45.290 --> 00:53:51.010
He says he'll run it over to Toby and the accounting department, so it'll just be, like, totally...

1080
00:53:51.010 --> 00:53:51.630
Impartial.

1081
00:53:51.770 --> 00:53:52.530
They'll run the data.

1082
00:53:52.730 --> 00:53:53.290
Exactly.

1083
00:53:53.630 --> 00:53:54.450
Just database.

1084
00:53:54.950 --> 00:53:55.170
Yeah.

1085
00:54:02.730 --> 00:54:06.010
Nothing into the kitchen is to get something out of the fridge, to run into Jim.

1086
00:54:06.350 --> 00:54:06.870
No.

1087
00:54:07.090 --> 00:54:07.790
What was it?

1088
00:54:08.210 --> 00:54:08.730
Mustard.

1089
00:54:09.130 --> 00:54:10.490
What's he doing with mustard?

1090
00:54:10.750 --> 00:54:11.570
I don't know.

1091
00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:12.930
Did he do anything with it?

1092
00:54:12.970 --> 00:54:16.810
He does nothing with the mustard, but when they pull back wide, there are more things

1093
00:54:16.810 --> 00:54:19.330
on the counter that he has gotten out of the fridge.

1094
00:54:19.750 --> 00:54:24.010
He also got out jelly and, I think, barbecue sauce.

1095
00:54:24.390 --> 00:54:24.910
Gross.

1096
00:54:25.290 --> 00:54:30.030
So he's definitely, I guess, heavy in the condiments.

1097
00:54:30.030 --> 00:54:32.630
Is jelly a condiment?

1098
00:54:33.090 --> 00:54:34.610
Is jelly a condiment?

1099
00:54:34.830 --> 00:54:36.450
I mean, you put it on...

1100
00:54:36.530 --> 00:54:37.550
Is butter a condiment?

1101
00:54:37.870 --> 00:54:38.790
I'm going to say no.

1102
00:54:38.790 --> 00:54:39.810
What is a condiment?

1103
00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:42.230
Isn't a condiment like...

1104
00:54:42.230 --> 00:54:44.210
Well, I guess, isn't it like the extras?

1105
00:54:44.210 --> 00:54:45.290
So maybe you're right.

1106
00:54:45.570 --> 00:54:48.770
I mean, what is a condiment?

1107
00:54:49.090 --> 00:54:50.250
Like salt and pepper?

1108
00:54:50.590 --> 00:54:51.730
No, that's a spice.

1109
00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:55.090
I'm sorry.

1110
00:54:55.790 --> 00:55:01.530
The definition of condiment is a substance such as salt or ketchup that is used to add

1111
00:55:01.530 --> 00:55:02.710
flavor to food.

1112
00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:03.530
Thank you.

1113
00:55:03.730 --> 00:55:05.390
Spices are condiments?

1114
00:55:05.750 --> 00:55:07.850
Look who was right about something.

1115
00:55:08.310 --> 00:55:12.710
I found a chart that lists the calories in different condiments.

1116
00:55:12.890 --> 00:55:15.510
Did you know honey is considered a condiment?

1117
00:55:15.730 --> 00:55:16.830
Well, that makes sense now.

1118
00:55:17.470 --> 00:55:20.290
They're listing marinara as a condiment.

1119
00:55:20.510 --> 00:55:21.890
It enhances flavor.

1120
00:55:22.590 --> 00:55:24.530
Everything enhances flavor.

1121
00:55:24.530 --> 00:55:26.910
An onion enhances flavor.

1122
00:55:27.130 --> 00:55:27.470
Garlic.

1123
00:55:28.230 --> 00:55:30.130
Does it have to be...

1124
00:55:30.130 --> 00:55:32.070
Do you have to squeeze it out of a bottle?

1125
00:55:32.270 --> 00:55:32.990
Does it have to be...

1126
00:55:32.990 --> 00:55:33.130
Yeah.

1127
00:55:33.510 --> 00:55:34.610
Does it have to be in something?

1128
00:55:35.570 --> 00:55:37.070
What is a condiment?

1129
00:55:37.450 --> 00:55:39.390
I need more information.

1130
00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:41.330
We need more information on condiments.

1131
00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:42.910
Hold on.

1132
00:55:43.230 --> 00:55:46.330
Is jelly a condiment?

1133
00:55:47.870 --> 00:55:53.510
Jelly and jam are usually viewed as condiments, but peanut butter is not.

1134
00:55:54.770 --> 00:55:59.390
Salsa is considered a condiment, but guacamole is not.

1135
00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:00.310
Here's what I'm thinking.

1136
00:56:00.470 --> 00:56:04.810
Condiments either have to be something you sprinkle or like kind of more of a liquidy form.

1137
00:56:05.230 --> 00:56:11.850
Not according to Wikipedia, which says a condiment is a supplemental food such as a sauce or powder.

1138
00:56:13.230 --> 00:56:14.430
Sprinkle and liquid.

1139
00:56:15.230 --> 00:56:16.610
That supports my theory.

1140
00:56:17.710 --> 00:56:20.230
If it's more of a solid substance, it's not a condiment.

1141
00:56:20.510 --> 00:56:22.690
If you can sprinkle it or pour it, it's a condiment.

1142
00:56:22.690 --> 00:56:24.650
If you can chop it, it's not a condiment.

1143
00:56:25.330 --> 00:56:26.350
Sprinkle and pour.

1144
00:56:26.910 --> 00:56:27.530
All right.

1145
00:56:27.710 --> 00:56:29.830
Are we ready to move on from the condiments?

1146
00:56:30.790 --> 00:56:31.230
Maybe.

1147
00:56:31.670 --> 00:56:32.090
Oh, no.

1148
00:56:33.830 --> 00:56:35.370
My God, you're making a lot of...

1149
00:56:35.370 --> 00:56:36.290
It sounds like...

1150
00:56:36.290 --> 00:56:36.610
You know what?

1151
00:56:36.730 --> 00:56:37.270
I'm sorry.

1152
00:56:37.270 --> 00:56:37.870
I'm sorry.

1153
00:56:37.990 --> 00:56:39.950
When you do that, hmm, it means you disagree.

1154
00:56:40.350 --> 00:56:40.770
Like, hmm.

1155
00:56:41.550 --> 00:56:42.030
Icing.

1156
00:56:43.110 --> 00:56:44.630
Icing is a condiment?

1157
00:56:44.950 --> 00:56:49.150
So when I'm eating a cupcake, I'm eating cake with a condiment on top?

1158
00:56:49.290 --> 00:56:50.250
Makes sense to me.

1159
00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:51.290
Really?

1160
00:56:51.830 --> 00:56:52.810
Jenna's doing it.

1161
00:56:52.930 --> 00:56:53.190
Hmm.

1162
00:56:53.470 --> 00:56:54.390
I knew it.

1163
00:56:56.560 --> 00:57:03.100
If you're ever in a conversation with Jenna and she goes, hmm, it means she is slightly judging you and disagrees.

1164
00:57:03.460 --> 00:57:05.740
Well, Wikipedia says peanut butter is a condiment.

1165
00:57:05.740 --> 00:57:08.020
So clearly, I don't know.

1166
00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:09.340
Clearly, there's a debate.

1167
00:57:09.860 --> 00:57:13.980
So Dwight has put a bunch of random condiments on the counter.

1168
00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:14.760
He has.

1169
00:57:15.260 --> 00:57:16.880
And I have nowhere to go from there.

1170
00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:17.640
That's the end.

1171
00:57:18.400 --> 00:57:19.480
We can move on.

1172
00:57:19.660 --> 00:57:22.100
He clearly isn't intending on doing anything with them.

1173
00:57:22.180 --> 00:57:25.620
He's just doing busy work so he can set up gym to fail.

1174
00:57:26.040 --> 00:57:30.160
Next up, Michael and Erin are going to arrive at the high school.

1175
00:57:30.300 --> 00:57:33.040
And I have a little location breakdown for you.

1176
00:57:33.040 --> 00:57:40.040
So fan question from Cyrus in Bloomfield, Michigan, were the school scenes filmed at a local L.A.

1177
00:57:40.180 --> 00:57:40.460
school?

1178
00:57:41.140 --> 00:57:42.240
Yes, they were.

1179
00:57:42.540 --> 00:57:50.580
We shot the interiors and exteriors at Van Nuys Middle School, which is a steam magnet school on Vesper Avenue in Van Nuys, California.

1180
00:57:51.120 --> 00:57:55.920
We were there for two days, Monday and Tuesday, October 3rd and 4th.

1181
00:57:55.920 --> 00:58:01.400
By the way, this is also where they shot the school location for the TV show Fresh Off the Boat.

1182
00:58:01.700 --> 00:58:04.200
Well, I had a recurring guest star on Fresh Off the Boat.

1183
00:58:04.620 --> 00:58:06.240
Do you know if you ever went to the school?

1184
00:58:06.440 --> 00:58:09.200
I think we did one day because I was at a PTA meeting.

1185
00:58:09.480 --> 00:58:13.300
I was like the mean lady shocker.

1186
00:58:15.460 --> 00:58:18.540
Well, there's a lot of great little details at this school.

1187
00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:20.020
Phil Shea outdid himself.

1188
00:58:20.200 --> 00:58:23.440
I loved the plaque that said Michael Gary Scott Reading Room.

1189
00:58:23.980 --> 00:58:25.900
And Erin's like, you're famous.

1190
00:58:26.320 --> 00:58:26.760
Yes.

1191
00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:33.160
Michael has cold feet about seeing everyone, but Mikayla greets them and insists they just want to give them a big thank you.

1192
00:58:33.400 --> 00:58:34.080
I know.

1193
00:58:34.700 --> 00:58:36.980
Mikayla was played by Eshiba Renee.

1194
00:58:37.520 --> 00:58:41.920
She posted on Instagram that she still has her shirt from this episode.

1195
00:58:41.920 --> 00:58:44.020
Oh, she got to keep her Scott's Tots shirt.

1196
00:58:44.080 --> 00:58:44.840
That's so cool.

1197
00:58:45.000 --> 00:58:46.000
She was so good.

1198
00:58:46.300 --> 00:58:53.880
And she is going to lead Michael into this classroom full of kids all wearing Scott's Tots shirts.

1199
00:58:54.260 --> 00:58:55.920
I mean, huge applause.

1200
00:58:56.140 --> 00:58:57.540
They're so excited he's there.

1201
00:58:57.860 --> 00:59:05.980
We find out that they have prepared something for him, which is a song and dance routine.

1202
00:59:06.700 --> 00:59:10.580
We had a fan question from Harmjen V in the Netherlands.

1203
00:59:11.220 --> 00:59:14.640
The dance routine of the Tots is awesome.

1204
00:59:15.340 --> 00:59:17.620
Where did you find these actors' dancers?

1205
00:59:18.040 --> 00:59:21.980
And Lisa from New Jersey asked who choreographed the dance routine.

1206
00:59:22.460 --> 00:59:26.600
Well, Randy Cordray shared with us that the choreographer was Tony Gonzalez.

1207
00:59:27.200 --> 00:59:31.820
And he had done choreography for Bring It On Again and Bring It On All or Nothing.

1208
00:59:31.820 --> 00:59:35.540
You know, he's currently doing the choreography for The Masked Singer.

1209
00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:39.320
And he cast the eight specialty dancers.

1210
00:59:39.980 --> 00:59:42.820
The dancers were Danielle E.

1211
00:59:42.920 --> 00:59:51.780
Hawkins, Glenda Morales, David Easel, Katrina Norman, Chris Moss, Oscar Orozco, Jonathan Rice, and Quentin Burdett.

1212
00:59:51.780 --> 00:59:55.320
But we only had two days of rehearsals with the dancers.

1213
00:59:55.860 --> 00:59:59.320
They set up a rehearsal space over on our warehouse stage.

1214
00:59:59.740 --> 01:00:01.100
They were so good.

1215
01:00:01.260 --> 01:00:02.340
They were so good.

1216
01:00:02.560 --> 01:00:07.200
There's no way in a million years that I could learn anything like that in two days.

1217
01:00:07.620 --> 01:00:08.080
No.

1218
01:00:08.320 --> 01:00:09.220
It's just a gift.

1219
01:00:09.400 --> 01:00:10.120
You just have it.

1220
01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:13.300
You know, I danced growing up.

1221
01:00:13.620 --> 01:00:16.100
There was a time when I could do that.

1222
01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:17.620
That time is not now anymore.

1223
01:00:18.340 --> 01:00:21.840
I tried during the pandemic to take up my tap dancing again.

1224
01:00:22.120 --> 01:00:23.520
I got a little tap dance floor.

1225
01:00:23.720 --> 01:00:24.180
I remember this.

1226
